summary:
- topic: Farewell to Board Member Claire Holman
  text: The board and Mayor Tipton honored outgoing member Claire Holman for her six
    years of service. Colleagues praised her passion, humor, and unwavering focus
    on student needs, while Holman encouraged citizens to run for public office and
    advocate for lowering the voting age to 16.
- topic: Recognition of South Portland Marching Band
  text: The board celebrated the South Portland Marching Band for receiving a gold
    medal at their state finals competition. Band Director Jen Fletcher highlighted
    the dedication of the 54 students, nearly half of whom were new to the activity
    this year, and thanked the community for their support.
- topic: Superintendent's Report and Reconfiguration Recommendations
  text: Superintendent Dr. Atkinson Duina presented an update on district strengths
    and challenges, enrollment, and the successful implementation of the new cell
    phone policy. Citing the need for stability, she recommended against a major elementary
    reconfiguration, instead proposing to explore an early learning center, create
    a committee for differential school funding, and commission an external review
    of special education programming.
- topic: Public Comment on AI Moratorium
  text: A proposed moratorium on AI use in K-8 grades drew significant public comment.
    Several teachers and librarians argued against it, stating it would disrupt established
    curriculum on media literacy and that educators need support and training, not
    a ban. Conversely, some parents, students, and a newly elected board member supported
    the pause, citing concerns about AI's impact on cognitive development and the
    quality of AI-generated curriculum.
- topic: Public Comment on Kaler School and MTSS Staffing
  text: Multiple community members and teachers from Protect South Portland raised
    serious health and safety concerns about reopening Kaler School as an early learning
    center, citing its proximity to oil tanks and new data on benzene emissions. Additionally,
    several teachers from Skillin Elementary detailed significant inequities in the
    allocation of MTSS staff and resources compared to other elementary schools, urging
    the board to address the disparities.
- topic: Board Rejects AI Moratorium
  text: Following extensive debate, the board voted 4-1 to reject the AI moratorium
    resolution. Most members, while acknowledging the concerns, preferred to wait
    for the administration's AI Steering Committee to present its comprehensive report
    and recommendations in January, expressing trust in the educators and the established
    process.
- topic: Board Adopts Superintendent's Recommendations
  text: The board unanimously approved moving forward with the superintendent's key
    recommendations. These actions include developing a proposal for an early learning
    center, establishing an ad hoc committee to explore differential funding for schools
    with higher needs, and commissioning an external review of the district's special
    education programming.
